scale-dependence-bf70f781.conceptual_definition:
The formula 
$\mathcal{L}_{eff} = \sum c_i \mathcal{O}_i \text{ (EFT Expansion)}$
 represents an effective Lagrangian (

$\mathcal{L}_{eff}$
) in the context of Effective Field Theory (EFT). An EFT is a theoretical framework used to describe physical phenomena at a particular energy or distance scale, without needing to know the details of the underlying theory at much shorter distances or higher energies. This formula expresses the effective Lagrangian as an infinite sum of operators (

$\mathcal{O}_i$
), each multiplied by a corresponding coefficient (

$c_i$
). The higher-order operators (those involving more fields or derivatives) are typically suppressed by powers of the ratio of the low-energy scale to the high-energy scale at which the EFT breaks down. Therefore, at low energies, only the first few terms in the expansion provide significant contributions.

